      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between a hammer drill and a drill driver?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A drill driver only rotates. A hammer drill rotates and simultaneously punches the bit forward thousands of times a minute, chipping brittle material so the flutes can clear it. In wood and metal the hammering is useless; in brick and concrete it is the only thing that makes progress.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a regular drill go through brick or concrete?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Into soft brick or mortar, slowly and with a masonry bit, sometimes yes. Into concrete, realistically no. Without percussion you are relying on abrasion alone, which overheats the bit, takes many times longer and often ends with a blunt bit and a shallow hole.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a hammer drill be used as a normal drill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Every hammer drill has a mode selector that switches the percussion off, at which point it behaves as an ordinary drill driver. That is why a combi drill is such a sensible single-tool purchase for a house with masonry walls.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between a hammer drill and a rotary hammer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A hammer drill uses two ridged clutch plates that click past each other, producing many light blows. A rotary hammer uses a piston driving air against a striker, producing far fewer but vastly more powerful blows. For anything beyond occasional small holes in concrete, the rotary hammer is a different class of tool.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need special bits for a hammer drill?",
