        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost everyone discovers this distinction the same way: leaning hard on a perfectly good drill, watching smoke rise from a masonry bit, and getting six millimetres into a wall in five minutes. The tool was not underpowered. It was the wrong kind of tool.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your walls are brick, block or concrete, you need percussion. A drill driver alone will not make holes in masonry at any reasonable speed, and no amount of pressing harder changes that. If your walls are timber studs and plasterboard, a plain drill driver is all you will ever use.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Principle</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Wood and metal are cut. Masonry is not cut — it is shattered, then swept away.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A twist bit slices a spiral shaving out of timber. A masonry bit has a blunt carbide tip that pulverises brittle material with repeated blows, and the flutes exist only to clear the dust. Rotation without impact gives it nothing to sweep out.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For most households the practical answer is a combi drill — a drill driver with a hammer mode you can switch off. It costs slightly more than a plain drill and removes an entire category of frustration.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mechanism" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Percussion Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            The mechanism inside a hammer drill is cleverer and cruder than people expect. Behind the chuck sit two ridged steel plates, rather like a pair of ratchet faces. One is fixed to the housing and one turns with the spindle.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you press the drill against a wall, the spindle is pushed back so the ridges engage. As it rotates, each ridge rides up over the opposing one and then drops off it, driving the chuck sharply forward and letting it spring back — thousands of times a minute. The result is a rapid forward chattering superimposed on the rotation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two consequences follow. First, the hammering only happens while you press: release the pressure and the plates disengage and the tool simply spins. Second, the blows are numerous but individually light, because the energy comes from your body weight pushing the tool rather than from a powered striker.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why a hammer drill is excellent in brick, mortar and lightweight block, and only adequate in concrete. Brick shatters readily under light repeated blows. Structural concrete with hard aggregate needs considerably more energy per impact than a clutch-plate mechanism can produce, which is where the rotary hammer comes in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="three-tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Three Tools, Not Two
          </h2>

          <p>
            The aisle actually contains three related tools, and confusing the second and third is the most expensive mistake available here.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The drill driver</strong> rotates only. It drills wood, metal and plastic, drives screws, and has an adjustable clutch. It is the default household power tool and covers most jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The hammer drill, or combi drill,</strong> is a drill driver with the percussion plates added and a mode selector to disable them. It handles brick, mortar and block confidently, and concrete slowly. It keeps a normal keyless chuck, so it takes all your existing bits.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The rotary hammer</strong> is a different machine that looks superficially similar. Instead of clutch plates it uses a piston driving a cushion of air against a striker, which slams into the back of the bit. That produces far fewer blows per minute but each carries many times the energy, and the energy comes from the motor rather than from you leaning on it. Rotary hammers use SDS chucks, where the bit slides and locks into the spindle rather than being clamped by jaws.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical difference is dramatic. A hole in structural concrete that takes a hammer drill several minutes and a ruined bit takes a small rotary hammer perhaps fifteen seconds. If you are fitting a few wall plugs a year, a combi drill is right. If you are anchoring anything into concrete regularly, an SDS rotary hammer is not a luxury.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Side-by-Side
          </h2>

