                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drill Driver</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hammer / Combi Drill</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Action</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotation only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotation plus forward percussion</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brick and mortar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slow and painful</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Confident and quick</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Effectively no</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Possible, small holes only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wood and metal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ideal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Identical, with hammer off</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lighter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slightly heavier and longer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Noise and vibration</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High in hammer mode</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chuck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keyless, takes any bit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Keyless, takes any bit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Modestly higher</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The table makes the buying decision fairly obvious. A combi drill does everything a drill driver does, identically, plus one more thing, for a small premium and a little extra weight. Unless every wall in your life is plasterboard, that is a straightforward trade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-material" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Tool by Material
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Material</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tool and Mode</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Softwood and hardwood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill driver, rotation only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plasterboard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill driver, low speed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Steel and aluminium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drill driver, slow, with cutting oil</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mortar joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hammer mode — easiest masonry there is</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hammer mode, masonry bit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lightweight block</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotation only is often enough</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Poured or structural concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotary hammer with SDS</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ceramic or porcelain tile</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotation only, tile or diamond bit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Glass and mirror</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotation only, spear point, water cooled</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Natural stone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies — start rotation only, diamond bit</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The aerated block row surprises people. Lightweight blocks are soft enough that percussion often does more harm than good, blowing out an oversized ragged hole that no fixing will grip. Try rotation first and only add hammering if you are making no progress.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before drilling any wall, know what is behind it. Cables and pipes run in predictable zones around sockets and switches, and a percussion blow through a cable is a serious matter. Our guide to the <a href="/best-stud-finder/">best stud finders</a> covers the detectors that find live wiring and metal as well as timber.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Bits Decide More Than the Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            More failed masonry holes are caused by the wrong or worn-out bit than by the wrong drill. The two bit types are not remotely interchangeable.
          </p>

          <p>
            A twist bit for wood or metal has sharp cutting edges arranged to slice material away. Put one against brick and those fine edges are destroyed within seconds, because masonry is harder than the steel and full of abrasive aggregate.
          </p>

          <p>
            A masonry bit has a blunt, wedge-shaped tip of tungsten carbide brazed onto a softer steel body. It is not sharp and is not meant to be: its job is to crush and chip whatever it is hammered into, while the wide, shallow flutes carry dust out of the hole. Carbide is very hard and very brittle, which is why it works under compression and fails if the bit overheats or is levered sideways.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two practical points follow. Masonry bits go blunt, and a blunt one polishes rather than chips — if a bit stops producing dust and starts producing smoke, it is finished, and pressing harder will only overheat it further and detach the carbide. And a hot bit is a dying bit: back out periodically to clear dust and let it cool, particularly on deeper holes.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you buy a rotary hammer, its SDS bits are a separate system again, with a splined shank that locks into the tool. They cannot be used in a normal chuck, and normal masonry bits cannot be used in an SDS chuck without an adapter that largely defeats the point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Technique That Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Masonry rewards a different technique from wood, and most of it is counterintuitive if you are used to timber.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start without hammering. Mark the spot, run the drill in rotation only at low speed to scratch a locating dimple, then engage hammer mode. Starting in percussion makes the bit skate across the surface and leaves a scarred wall around a hole that is not where you wanted it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then apply firm, steady pressure and keep the drill square to the surface. The percussion mechanism only engages under load, so a tentative touch produces rotation without hammering and gets nowhere. Equally, leaning with your whole body does not help beyond a point and makes it harder to hold the tool straight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Withdraw the bit periodically to clear dust. A hole packed with masonry dust stops the bit reaching fresh material, generates heat and slows progress dramatically — pulling out every centimetre or so on a deep hole is quicker overall despite feeling like an interruption.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, wear eye protection and a dust mask. Masonry drilling throws sharp fragments and produces fine silica dust, which is genuinely harmful to breathe. On more than a couple of holes indoors, holding a vacuum nozzle near the bit keeps both your lungs and the carpet in better condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most households, a cordless combi drill is the right answer. It is a full drill driver with percussion available when needed, it takes ordinary bits, and the premium over a plain drill driver is small. Our <a href="/best-cordless-drills/">cordless drill reviews</a> and our guide to <a href="/best-cordless-drill-for-home-use/">drills for home use</a> cover models with and without the hammer function.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy a plain drill driver only if you are certain masonry is not in your future — a timber-framed house with no concrete floor or garden walls — or if you already own a rotary hammer and want the lightest possible tool for everything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy a rotary hammer if you are anchoring into structural concrete more than occasionally: mounting a TV on a concrete wall, fixing a gate post, installing shelving in a basement, or any repeated work in a slab. For a one-off job, hiring one for a day costs a fraction of buying and will do in an hour what a combi drill fails to do all afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            As always, the battery platform outlives the tool. If you already own cordless tools, staying within that family is usually worth more than a small specification advantage elsewhere — a point our <a href="/cordless-tool-batteries-explained/">battery guide</a> and our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">platform comparison</a> both make in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Corded Still Has a Place</h2>
        <p>
          Cordless has taken over almost everything, but masonry is the job where a cord still earns its keep. Percussion drilling draws heavy sustained current, and a series of holes in brick will empty a battery noticeably faster than the same amount of work in timber.
        </p>
        <p>
          A corded hammer drill costs a fraction of a comparable cordless one, never runs out mid-hole, and does not care how long the job takes. For someone who drills masonry rarely but wants the capability available, an inexpensive corded hammer drill kept alongside a cordless driver is a genuinely sensible combination.
        </p>
        <p>
          The counter-argument is that the tool you actually reach for is the one that is charged and to hand. Many people find a cordless combi drill gets used and a corded one stays in the cupboard, which is a real consideration — the best tool for a job is not much use if fetching an extension lead persuades you to use the wrong one instead.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Twist Bit on Masonry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wood or metal bit's fine cutting edges are destroyed within seconds against brick, and it will never make the hole. Masonry needs a blunt carbide tip designed to crush rather than cut, and no amount of pressure substitutes for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting in Hammer Mode</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Percussion from the first moment makes the bit skate across the surface, scarring the wall and putting the hole off the mark. Scratch a locating dimple in rotation only, then switch the hammering on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hammering Through Tiles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Percussion cracks glazed tiles, often invisibly at first and then across the whole face. Go through the tile in rotation only with a tile or diamond bit, and engage hammering only once you reach the masonry behind.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressing Harder on a Blunt Bit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When a masonry bit stops producing dust and starts producing smoke, it is worn out. More pressure only overheats it further and can detach the carbide tip. Replace the bit — they are cheap and the difference is night and day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drilling Blind Into a Wall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cables and pipes run in predictable zones around sockets and switches, and a percussion blow through either is serious. Use a detector that finds live wiring and metal, and keep clear of the vertical and horizontal zones extending from fittings.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a hammer drill and a drill driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A drill driver only rotates. A hammer drill rotates and punches the bit forward thousands of times a minute, chipping brittle material so the flutes can clear it. In wood and metal that hammering is useless; in brick and concrete it is the whole job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a regular drill go through brick or concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Into soft brick or mortar, slowly, with a masonry bit — sometimes. Into concrete, realistically no. Without percussion you rely on abrasion alone, which overheats the bit, takes many times longer and usually ends in a blunt bit and a shallow hole.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a hammer drill be used as a normal drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every hammer drill has a mode selector that switches percussion off, after which it behaves exactly as an ordinary drill driver. That is why a combi drill is such a sensible single-tool purchase for a house with masonry walls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a hammer drill and a rotary hammer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A hammer drill uses ridged clutch plates clicking past each other for many light blows. A rotary hammer uses a piston driving air against a striker for far fewer, vastly more powerful blows. Beyond occasional small holes in concrete, it is a different class of tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need special bits for a hammer drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes — masonry bits with a carbide tip brazed to the end. Standard wood or metal twist bits are destroyed almost immediately. Rotary hammers need SDS bits, which lock into their own chuck rather than being gripped by jaws.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a hammer drill worth buying for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your home has brick, block or concrete, a combi drill costs only a little more than a plain drill driver and removes an entire category of frustration. If everything is timber-framed with plasterboard, the hammer function will rarely be used.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my hammer drill not going into the concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually a blunt or wrong bit, hammer mode not properly engaged, too little forward pressure, or you have hit rebar. Hammer drills also struggle in hard aggregate-rich concrete, which is exactly where a rotary hammer becomes necessary.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a hammer drill on tiles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No — percussion cracks glazed tiles. Drill through the tile in rotation-only mode with a tile or diamond bit, then switch hammering on once you are through the tile and into the masonry behind it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

