        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A drill driver cuts; a hammer drill shatters. Wood and metal are sliced away by sharp edges, while masonry has to be pulverised by repeated forward blows and swept out as dust. Rotation alone gives a masonry bit nothing to clear, which is why a perfectly good drill makes almost no progress in brick.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most homes a combi drill is the right buy: everything a drill driver does, plus percussion when you need it, for a small premium. Step up to an SDS rotary hammer if structural concrete features regularly in your work, and hire one rather than suffering for a single job.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then remember that bits matter as much as tools. Carbide-tipped masonry bits, replaced when they stop making dust, start the hole in rotation only, and clear the dust as you go — those four habits turn masonry drilling from a fight into a routine task.
          </p>
